mm.\n\nI think my visual estimates are unreliable. Let me instead systematically query the actual nametable around the visible region. Since camera=293 (page 1, low 37), the visible area is nametable 1 from x=37 to 37+256=293 in nametable-1 coords. So the on-screen tiles correspond to nametable 1 columns 4..36 (tile_x = (37..293)/8 = 4.6..36.6).\n\nLet me read nametable 1 region x=4..19, y=0..29 maybe. Actually read_nametable 1 (x=4,y=0) 32x30 is too big (max 32x30). Let me do x=4, y=10, width=32, height=20. That covers the visible playfield. Hmm, but width max 32 ok.\n\nActually the nametable read gives tile IDs at nametable positions; the visible on-screen x = tile_x*8 - 37. Let me do read_nametable 1 x=0 y=0 32x30? Max 32 wide, 30 tall. That's the whole nametable — 32x30. Let me just do it; output will be large but structured as rows.\n\nActually the tool returns a grid; might be big but manageable.\n\nLet me do read_nametable(0, ...) hmm which nametable? get_tile_at_pixel says nametable 1 for most. At camera 293, the screen shows nametable 1 (256..511) and possibly nametable 2 (512..767) at the right edge? Screen right = 293+256=549 > 512, so the right ~37px shows nametable 2. Hmm, but these are mirror nametables; the game uses 0 and 1 only typically, then scrolls back. Actually SMB uses nametables 0 and 1 for the level (two 256px pages), then reuses them.\n\nAnyway, let me read nametable 1 x=0 y=0 32x30 to see the layout around here, focusing on rows 18-23 and 14-15.\n\nHmm, this is a lot. Let me just do it.\n\nActually, maybe simpler: The key deliverable is the fire flower and fireball. Let me not over-invest in perfect level knowledge. \n\nLet me reconsider: We're big, at level x=434, stopped. Ahead: the screenshot shows a pipe. Hmm, actually if Mario is stopped at 434, maybe he's stopped by the brick row's right end? No.\n\nWait, actually, maybe the reason he stopped is that he's at the right edge of the \"page\" and the level scrolling paused? No, camera also stopped at 37.\n\nHmm, maybe he's blocked by a solid object: there's a \"brick\" at ground level? Or maybe the ? block at (85,122) is actually at Mario's head level and he's stopped by a wall? No, walls don't stop horizontal at that height.\n\nActually! Maybe he stopped because a Goomba/Koopa is in front and he's \"pushing\" against it? No, SMB Mario doesn't stop on enemies.\n\nHmm, maybe he stopped because he reached a pipe and the pipe is at screen ~155? Let me re-look: get_tile_at_pixel(215,190) returned blank. So no pipe at 215. Where's the pipe? The screenshot shows green pipe at ~(205,185). Hmm, but blank there.\n\nMy screen-coordinate estimates from the image seem systematically off. The image is 256x240 but displayed; maybe the preview is scaled/padded. Let me instead trust the tools.\n\nget_tile_at_pixel(215,190) → screen_box (211,184), tile blank. So at screen 211,184 there's blank. The pipe green thing in the screenshot must be elsewhere. Hmm.\n\nActually wait — maybe the screenshot preview I'm seeing is at a different scale than 256x240. The tool says \"request preview 256x240px\". Should be 1:1.\n\nHmm, the mismatch is concerning. Let me get_pixel_color at a few points to cross-check.\n\nActually, let me reconsider get_tile_at_pixel's screen_box: for (150,155) it returned screen_box x=147,y=152 — meaning the requested pixel (150,155) falls in a tile whose screen box starts at (147,152). But 147 is not a multiple of 8. That's odd! Tile screen boxes should be multiples of 8. x=147 suggests the scroll offset is non-multiple (37). So screen_box = tile_x*8 - scroll + something. tile_x=18 → 144; -37 → 107. But it says 147. Hmm, 18*8=144, and screen_box 147 = 144+3. Weird.\n\nHmm, actually maybe the tool's screen_box uses the nametable pixel position minus scroll differently. Let me not trust screen_box for exact pixel mapping, but tile_id is the tile at that nametable cell. So at requested pixel (150,155) the tile is blank. But screenshot shows bricks around there. So the brick row must be at different y.\n\nWait — maybe my visual read of y is off because the screenshot includes the HUD. Hmm.\n\nLet me carefully re-describe screenshot #2: \n- Top: HUD MARIO 001200, coin x01, WORLD 1-1, TIME 100.\n- Small cloud at upper-left (~35,40), big cloud at upper-right (~160,40).\n- A lone brown ? block at around (80,118)?\n- Below it a row of bricks around y~150 from x~25 to x~110?\n- Mario (red) at ~(140,175).\n- A green pipe at ~(205,185)?\n- Green hill at left edge (~0,180).\n- Ground bricks bottom.\n\nHmm. But tools say (85,125) blank and (150,155) blank. \n\nLet me query a grid of pixels to find the actual ? block and bricks. Let me query (80,120),(80,130),(90,120),(90,130),(100,150),(100,160),(60,150),(40,150),(20,150),(200,190),(210,190),(220,190).\n\nLet me batch ~10 calls. Actually there might be a limit; let me do 6.\n\nAlternatively, read_nametable 1 is more efficient. Let me do read_nametable(1, x=0,y=12,width=32,height=12). That gives rows 12-23, covering y 96-191. Output: 12 rows of 32 ids. Manageable.\n\nLet me do that plus read_nametable(2,...) for right edge? Let's start with nametable 1.\n\nActually, the visible region: camera=293 → screen x 0 corresponds to nametable 1 pixel 37 → tile 4. Screen 256 → 549 → nametable 2 pixel 37. So the left ~219px is nametable 1 tiles 4-31, and the right ~37px is nametable 2 tiles 0-4. So I need both. Ugh.\n\nHmm, but actually maybe the game's level uses nametable 0 and 1, and at camera 293 it's showing... hmm. $071C=37, and nametable select? get_tile_at_pixel said nametable 1. And at x=215,y=190 it said tile_x=26, nametable 1 → nametable1 pixel 208 → level 256+208=464 → screen 464-293=171. But requested x=215. Off by 44. So indeed the right part uses a different nametable.\n\nI think the tool's screen_box is unreliable. Let me just use get_pixel_color + get_tile_at_pixel to identify objects by scanning.\n\nHmm, this is a slog. Let me step back and take the decisive pragmatic